UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 1: Persuasion in everyday texts', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Everyday texts such as notices, packaging and posts can persuade. Identify audience, purpose and techniques before accepting the message.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nFor a library poster \"Silent readers finish more quests\", name audience, purpose and one technique.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAudience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests).\n\nCHECK\nEveryday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCollect three everyday persuasive texts. For each, record audience, purpose, two techniques and one question a careful reader should ask.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Everyday texts such as notices, packaging and posts can persuade. Identify audience, purpose and techniques before accepting the message.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Collect three everyday persuasive texts. For each, record audience, purpose, two techniques and one question a careful reader should ask. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Audience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests). Reasoning: I used everyday persuasion by attending to purpose and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first. Correction: Audience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests). Why: the correction restores accurate use of everyday persuasion.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating everyday persuasion on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with everyday persuasion, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Audience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests). This matches the lesson skill for everyday persuasion.","options":["Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Audience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests)."],"question":"For a library poster \"Silent readers finish more quests\", name audience, purpose and one technique. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of purpose and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first.","Everyday texts such as notices, packaging and posts can persuade. Identify audience, purpose and techniques before accepting the message.","Audience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests)."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising everyday persuasion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named purpose and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the purpose and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising everyday persuasion. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries everyday persuasion.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show everyday persuasion and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first."],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses everyday persuasion from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Audience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests).","Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about everyday persuasion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise purpose and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about everyday persuasion.","Retype the worked model as the final answer."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of everyday persuasion to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating everyday persuasion on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Audience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests). Reasoning: I used everyday persuasion by attending to purpose and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with everyday persuasion, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first. Correction: Audience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests). Why: the correction restores accurate use of everyday persuasion.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w16-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise everyday persuasion.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use everyday persuasion with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify purpose and explain why it matters.","I can practise technique independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Everyday texts such as notices, packaging and posts can persuade. Identify audience, purpose and techniques before accepting the message. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Everyday texts such as notices, packaging and posts can persuade. Identify audience, purpose and techniques before accepting the message.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about everyday persuasion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA canteen notice \"Buy fruit first — fuel for learning!\" aims at students, uses a slogan and links fruit to energy.\n\nTRY FIRST\nFor a library poster \"Silent readers finish more quests\", name audience, purpose and one technique.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAudience: students using the library. Purpose: encourage quiet reading. Technique: linking silence to a reward idea (quests).","explain":"Everyday texts such as notices, packaging and posts can persuade. Identify audience, purpose and techniques before accepting the message.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Everyday persuasion can be useful or misleading; purpose recognition comes first. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Everyday persuasive canteen notice.","caption":"Find audience, purpose and technique.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\