 recognition comes first. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about everyday persuasion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEveryday texts such as notices, packaging and posts can persuade. Identify audience, purpose and techniques before accepting the message. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content